windows: commit call-return stacks and compile buffers as they are used

Two more things were committed in full for each emulated thread and barely used:

 - the call-return stack, 4 MB, of which a thread uses a few KB around its starting point;
 - the compile buffers a thread takes from the pools once it has translated something: 8 MB
   for IR, 16 MB for the backend's temporary code, and the decoder's buffer.

On macOS committed memory that something merely reads becomes real memory (see 0005, 0006), so
a game that reads through its memory paid 28 MB for each thread that had ever compiled.

Only the emulator's own code touches these, never a system call, so they can be committed by
the fault handler: FEXCore::Allocator::VirtualOvercommit is a hook the Windows frontend points
at its OvercommitTracker, which already commits the lookup tables 64 KB at a time. With the
hook set, the pools reserve their buffers and mark them (the guarded pool leaves its last
page unmarked, which makes it the guard without a protect call), and the call-return stack
commits 128 KB around its starting point and marks the rest. Clearing the stack no longer
commits all of it again. Without the hook everything is committed as before.

The WOW64 frontend asks the tracker before it asks the call-return stack, as the ARM64EC one
does, so that a marked part of the stack is committed and not taken for an imbalance.

Measured with 32 threads that each ran 24,000 blocks, reading all committed memory afterwards:
1655 MB before 0006 and this, 265 MB after (110 MB before the read). A fault costs about 5 us
and happens once for each 64 KB a thread comes to use. Compiling 48,000 blocks and starting
256 threads measure the same as before.

--- a/FEXCore/include/FEXCore/Utils/AllocatorHooks.h
+++ b/FEXCore/include/FEXCore/Utils/AllocatorHooks.h
@@ -103,6 +103,40 @@
 
 FEX_DEFAULT_VISIBILITY extern VirtualNamePtr VirtualName;
 FEX_DEFAULT_VISIBILITY extern VirtualTHPPtr VirtualTHPControl;
+
+// FineWine: memory that only the emulator's own code touches can be left reserved and committed
+// by the frontend's fault handler as it is used (64 KB at a time), instead of all at once.
+// Committed memory can be read, and on macOS memory that is only read still becomes real memory,
+// so a buffer of 8 or 16 MB that a thread uses a few KB of should not be committed whole.
+// The frontend sets VirtualOvercommit to mark (true) or unmark (false) such a range; while it is
+// unset the memory is committed in full as before. Never use this for memory that is passed to
+// a system call: Wine's side of the call does not go through the fault handler.
+using VirtualOvercommitPtr = void (*)(void* Ptr, size_t Size, bool Mark);
+FEX_DEFAULT_VISIBILITY extern VirtualOvercommitPtr VirtualOvercommit;
+
+// GuardSize bytes at the end are never committed, so touching them always faults.
+inline void* VirtualAllocLazy(size_t Size, size_t GuardSize = 0) {
+  const auto Hook = VirtualOvercommit;
+  if (!Hook) {
+    return VirtualAlloc(Size);
+  }
+  void* Ptr = VirtualAlloc(Size, false, false);
+  if (Ptr) {
+    Hook(Ptr, Size - GuardSize, true);
+  }
+  return Ptr;
+}
+
+inline void VirtualFreeLazy(void* Ptr, size_t Size, size_t GuardSize = 0) {
+  if (const auto Hook = VirtualOvercommit) {
+    Hook(Ptr, Size - GuardSize, false);
+  }
+  VirtualFree(Ptr, Size);
+}
+
+inline bool VirtualIsLazy() {
+  return VirtualOvercommit != nullptr;
+}
